# kano-proxy

Canonical project rules (`.rule`; Cursor / AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md symlink here). This file keeps only the guardrails — `docs/` is the source of truth for details.

## Docs first

- Before non-trivial work, read `docs/index.md` and the linked docs matching the task.
- Feature/schema/behavior changes: update `docs/` first, then implement (small obvious fixes exempt). Keep `docs/index.md` paths and summaries in sync.
- If a request conflicts with docs, state the conflict and wait for docs or an explicit user override.

## Secrets and private context

- Never commit secrets (API tokens, upstream LLM keys, OAuth client secrets, session/cookie secrets). Real values live in `.dev.vars` / `wrangler secret` / CI secret stores; tracked files and docs use placeholders like `<upstream-api-key>`. Never print secret values — only whether one exists. If unsure whether a value is secret, treat it as secret.
- `.local/` (gitignored; committed template `.local.example/`) holds private ops context: real DNS, hostnames, route bind order, deploy notes. Read it before deploy/DNS/production work and prefer its facts; never copy its contents into tracked files, commits, or PR text. No tokens in `.local/` either. If it's missing, use placeholders and ask the user for private values.
- Committed `apps/api/wrangler.toml` keeps placeholder D1/KV ids and local `APP_URL`; real production ids/hosts go only in gitignored `wrangler.production.toml`. Deploy/migrate/secret with `--config wrangler.production.toml`.

## Stack

- Cloudflare by default: Workers (API/proxy, TypeScript), Pages (Vue 3 + Vite + TypeScript web), D1, KV, Durable Objects only if coordination requires. Single approved exception: Codex egress relay on Cloud Run (`docs/codex-relay.md`). Any other non-Cloudflare service or new primary language requires a docs rationale first.
- Environments: local + production only. No staging without explicit user approval and a docs update.
- D1 schema changes only via Wrangler migrations under `apps/api/migrations/`, docs (`docs/database.md`) updated first; never edit production schema in the Dashboard.
- Monorepo layout and module boundaries: `docs/project-structure.md`. Domains, DNS, Wrangler, release CI: `docs/deployment.md` (tracked docs keep placeholders; real DNS lives in `.local/dns.md`).
- Releases: production deploys happen only by publishing a GitHub Release — never auto-deploy from push to `main` or PRs. SemVer: tag `vX.Y.Z` must match root `package.json` `"version"`, bumped + committed + pushed before tagging; default bump is minor. Full flow: `docs/deployment.md`.
- Release notes are **hand-written** and passed with `--notes-file`; never publish with `--generate-notes` alone. It summarizes merged PRs, this repo lands direct commits, so it emits a bare compare link — and those notes are the only thing the in-app `/changelog` page has to show. Write for the operator, not the diff.

## Proxy guardrails

- Preserve end-to-end streaming (SSE/chunked); never buffer a whole upstream stream in memory.
- Clients authenticate with project-issued API keys or sessions — upstream provider keys stay in secrets and never reach clients.
- Rate limits and usage accounting fail closed for abuse but must not crash the Worker or wipe unrelated state.
- Do not log full prompts, completions, or secrets by default (`docs/logging.md`).
- No password login — admin sign-in is Google OIDC; API keys stored hashed at rest (`docs/auth.md`). API behavior, failover, pools: `docs/api.md`, `docs/providers.md`. Admin UI cache-first rules: `docs/admin-ui.md`.

## Cost safety — real upstream traffic is real money

- **Never** debug, reproduce, bisect, or benchmark by sending real requests through the deployed proxy or directly to paid upstreams; every such request bills the operator, and large-context probes against premium models cost dollars each. Verification is unit tests with stubbed `fetch`.
- A live smoke test needs the user's explicit per-instance approval and must be minimal: tiny prompt, `max_tokens` ≤ 32, no long-context/`[1M]` variants, never in a loop or size sweep.
- For production failures use free evidence first: `request_logs` in D1 (`wrangler d1 execute --remote`), `wrangler tail`, Cloudflare dashboards. Do not generate traffic to make logs appear.
- Treat paid upstream credit balances like production data: exhausting them is an outage.

## Real data only

- Never fabricate product data (models, accounts, usage, keys, profiles). Frontend renders only what the backend returns (empty is fine); backend never hard-codes model ids, emails, or usage as if live — source from upstream APIs or D1 rows created by real flows. If a live source is missing, return empty/`available: false` and document the gap.
- Allowed exceptions: unit-test fixtures/mocks in `tests/`, docs examples with explicit placeholders, pure UI chrome. Treat existing hard-coded product lists as debt: replace when touching that area.

## Misc

- Build/tool outputs stay gitignored; new tools with local outputs must update `.gitignore`. Do not delete ignored artifacts just to clean `git status` — only when asked, on errors, or for a verified clean rebuild (explain first).
- Fix clear typos in code identifiers/filenames when touching that code and report the rename; do not rename user-facing strings or deliberate spellings without asking.
